  "title": "Kourovka problem 20.19 (main)",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "pdf-text-entry",
    "text": "A subgroup H of a group G is called commensurated if for all g ∈ G, the\nindex |H : H ∩ gHg −1 | is finite. Can a non-abelian free group (or a non-elementary\nhyperbolic group) contain two infinite commensurated subgroups A, B with a trivial\nintersection? The answer is negative if A or B is normal.               P.-E. Caprace",
